Full report written to: …/children/worker_0/children/worker_2/outputs/facet.md
(~22 KB, 11 sections).

                                 Key findings

1. The canonical SKILL.md contract (derived from the only fully-shipped local
example ~/.agents/skills/twg/SKILL.md + AgentFoundation registry.py + OpenClaw
skill-*.rst docs):

 • A skill is a directory with SKILL.md (mandatory), references/*.md (optional
   ref set), scripts/<binary> (optional wrapper). Discovered via 6-layer
   precedence (~/.agents/skills/ = priority 4).
 • YAML frontmatter required: name, description (used as 150-char summary in
   the agent prompt), labels, compatibility, optional
   metadata.openclaw.{primaryEnv, emoji, os, 
   requires.{bins,anyBins,env,config}, install}.
 • Body sections in this exact order: Overview → Available scripts →
   Prerequisites → Workflow (numbered, ≤8 steps, "Verb — explanation." form) →
   Guidance nudges → Common patterns (fenced bash) → Advanced (escape hatch) →
   References.
 • Budget: ≤ 6 KB SKILL.md (hard caps at 30 KB/skill, 256 KB/file).
 • Path-resolution caveat is a required sentence whenever scripts/<x> is
   referenced.

2. The canonical tool.json contract (derived from
ToolDefinition/ParameterDef/SubcommandDef in models.py + 20 example tool.json
files):

 • Fields: name (snake_case), tool_type (Action|Conversation), category
   (workflow|knowledge|session|utility|conversation), aliases, is_bridge,
   asynchronous, description, parameters, subcommands, returns, examples,
   usage_guidance, agent_enabled, executor (dotted import).
 • Parameter conventions: long-form flags only, positional first, type ∈
   {string, int, flag, path}, choices enum for finite domains, JSON-string
   idiom for multi-key flags (--params '{"k":"v"}').
 • Subcommands: single level only (cf. knowledge with 11 verbs). Recommended
   verbs for Build: start_run/log_*/search_runs/end_run/register_model
   (mlflow), submit_run/launch_hpo/poll/cancel (mlstudio),
   audit_*/bulk_audit/apply_plan (tecton).

3. The scripts/<tool> wrapper idiom (from ~/.agents/skills/twg/scripts/twg):

 • set -euo pipefail, hardcoded binary path with fallback, --mode agent
   injection, JSON output by default for non-help calls, stdout/stderr split
   to /tmp/<tool>/<epoch>/run.XXX/{stdout[.json],stderr} with stdout=<path>
   lines printed back. Build wrappers must adopt this verbatim.

4. Naming: Model_<TS> = Model_<YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ> UTC; variant suffix __<slug>
(double underscore); ablation __abl_<component>. Tool/skill names: snake_case,
never plural; aliases terse and hyphenated.

5. AI-autonomy patterns observed:

 • Build skills should be model-invoked only (no user-invocable);
   deterministic dispatch would over-trigger.
 • Write-confirmation gates re-use existing confirm_action/confirmation tools
   with --yolo override (cf. /sop).
 • Executors return ToolResult dicts with success: bool + structured
   data/error — never raise.
 • Retry/backoff lives in executor.py, not in tool.json or SKILL.md.
 • Composition is done via sop (is_bridge: true, asynchronous: true); SKILL.md
   ## Workflow steps must be deterministic enough for a future /sop 
   model_build to step through.

6. Cross-tool nudges to encode:

 • "Avoid the switcheroo MCP for ML experiments — it is product feature flags,
   not ML."
 • "Avoid raw socrates MCP for feature audits when tecton_mlfs_client audit_*
   exists."
 • "Prefer typed wrappers over raw mlflow/databricks/tecton CLI."
 • "State a dry-run summary before any register_model/apply_plan/submit_run."

7. Day-One Readiness rows are scoped in the report:

 • mlflow_run_manager → MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI/DATABRICKS_TOKEN; MLflow scopes
   experiments:create,runs:*,models:register.
 • mlstudio_databricks_job_launcher → Databricks PAT scopes
   jobs:run,cluster-policies:read,clusters:create,unity-catalog:write; GPU 
   quota per cluster policy (e.g., g5.xlarge × N).
 • tecton_mlfs_client → TECTON_API_KEY/TECTON_CLUSTER_URL; scopes
   featureview:read|write,materialization:trigger.

8. Drop-in templates produced in the report: ml_experiment_lifecycle SKILL.md
skeleton (frontmatter + 8 sections) and mlflow_run_manager.tool.json skeleton
with full subcommand/parameter shapes the aggregator can copy and extend for
the other two tools.

9. Authoring checklists (§10 of report) — point-by-point validators the
aggregator can run against produced artifacts before finalizing.


10. Open questions for the aggregator: (a) whether the runtime enforces
metadata.openclaw.requires today (registry reads but doesn't gate), (b)
install location (<workspace>/.agents/skills/ vs packaged
src/agent_foundation/resources/skills/), (c) whether executors can live
outside agent_foundation.resources.tools.* (recommend: don't), (d) tool_type
admits only Action/Conversation in observed examples.
