  ['artifacts/building_ml_models_vnext.txt:\n\n````text\n   0 Executive         
  Summary This paper provides a comprehensive template for implementing         
  machine learning models in Atlassian's vNext platform with MLFlow             
  integration and automated deployment through Airflow. The implementation      
  demonstrates best practices for model tracking, parameter management,         
  automated forecasting, and production deployment using Prophet time series    
  models for business unit metrics forecasting. Machine learning model          
  development and deployment in enterprise environments requires robust         
  tracking, reproducibility, and automation. This implementation showcases a    
  complete ML pipeline built on Atlassian's vNext platform that: Trains         
  multiple Prophet time series models for business unit forecasting Tracks all  
  model parameters, metrics, and artifacts using MLFlow Automatically deploys   
  forecasts to production tables Runs on a scheduled basis through Airflow      
  orchestration Provides comprehensive model evaluation and performance         
  monitoring Key Business Impact: 30-day forecasts for 14 different queue       
  metrics across 3 business units Multiple aggregation types (total, chat,      
  non-chat) for comprehensive coverage Advanced weekday/weekend performance     
  analysis with conditional seasonalities sMAPE-based performance assessment    
  with automated quality tagging Automated model retraining and deployment      
  twice weekly Complete model lineage and experiment tracking Why MLFlow is     
  Critical The Challenge Without MLFlow image-20251016-194114.png Before        
  implementing MLFlow, ML model development typically suffers from: Experiment  
  Chaos : No systematic way to track which parameters produced which results    
  Reproducibility Issues : Inability to recreate successful model runs Model    
  Versioning Problems : No clear lineage between model versions and             
  performance Collaboration Difficulties : Teams can't easily share and         
  compare model experiments Production Deployment Risks : No clear path from    
  experiment to production MLFlow's Core Benefits Experiment Tracking # Every   
  model run is tracked with complete context with                               
  mlflow.start_run(run_name=f"Prophet_MultiAgg_{pd.Timestamp.now().strftime('%  
  Y%m%d_%H%M')}"): # All parameters, metrics, and artifacts automatically       
  logged mlflow.log_params(config_params)                                       
  mlflow.log_metrics(evaluation_metrics) mlflow.prophet.log_model(model,        
  f"prophet/{model_key}") MLFlow provides comprehensive experiment tracking     
  that captures the complete context of every model run. As demonstrated in     
  the code example, each training session is wrapped in a managed run context   
  that automatically logs all parameters, evaluation metrics, and model         
  artifacts. This systematic approach ensures that every hyperparameter         
  combination is recorded alongside its corresponding performance metrics,      
  creating a complete audit trail of the modeling process. Configuration files  
  are versioned and stored as artifacts, enabling detailed analysis of how      
  different parameter choices impact model performance across experiments.      
  Model Versioning and Lineage The framework establishes automatic versioning   
  for every model run through unique identifiers that maintain clear lineage    
  throughout the development lifecycle. Models are stored alongside any custom  
  associated artifacts you want to include ie (evaluation plots, feature        
  importance charts, and performance metrics) —creating a cohesive package      
  that documents both the model and its validation results. This unified        
  storage approach provides transparent connections between experimental        
  iterations and production deployments, allowing teams to trace any            
  production model back to its original development context and the specific    
  decisions that led to its deployment. Collaboration and Governance MLFlow's   
  centralized architecture enables seamless collaboration by providing          
  team-wide access to all model experiments and their outcomes. The integrated  
  model registry serves as a single source of truth, clearly identifying        
  production-ready models with appropriate versioning and metadata. This        
  shared infrastructure creates a comprehensive audit trail that documents the  
  complete history of model development and deployment decisions, supporting    
  both team coordination and organizational governance requirements. Teams can  
  efficiently compare experiments, reproduce results, and build upon previous   
  work without the fragmentation that typically hampers collaborative ML        
  development. Business Value Delivered Our implementation demonstrates         
  measurable benefits: Reduced Experiment Time : Much faster iteration through  
  systematic parameter tracking Improved Model Quality : Better model           
  selection through comprehensive evaluation metrics Enhanced Reproducibility   
  : 100% reproducible model runs through complete configuration logging         
  Advanced Performance Analysis : Weekday/weekend breakdowns with sMAPE         
  assessments for zero-value handling Automated Quality Assessment :            
  Text-based performance tagging (Excellent/Good/Acceptable/Poor/Very Poor)     
  Faster Deployment : Automated path from experiment to production deployment   
  Architecture Overview What problems am I trying to solve? Trying to build     
  and track lots of models. How can we recreate? See how models evolve over     
  time, how do we measure? How can we version? Which version produced which     
  results? Reduce the time to deploy models and land forecasts into something   
  accessible image-20251016-194034.png Data Ingestion : Automated data loading  
  from production tables with aggregated metrics Notebook: contains the         
  Prophet Model training and produces all of the model evaluation and multiple  
  metrics. MLFlow Tracking : houses all of the metrics, parameters, artifacts   
  and model registry. Prophet models trained and models are registered. with    
  MLFlow tracking Forecast Generation : once the models are trained, the        
  forecasts are generated and then landed in a table.                           
  image-20251007-165139.png Example of Mlflow with Metrics Parameters and       
  Tags. image-20251007-170028.png Forecast Table with Upper and Lower limits,   
  the forecast date and model version Implementation Components File Structure  
  Overview image-20251007-224423.png Core Implementation Files Main ML          
  Pipeline ( business_unit_metric_forecasts.ipynb ) The primary forecasting     
  pipeline implements a comprehensive multi-model training system that          
  systematically generates 126 individual Prophet models spanning the complete  
  forecasting matrix: three business units (Cloud SMB, Cloud Enterprise, and    
  Strategic Accounts), three aggregation types (total, chat, and non-chat       
  channels), and fourteen distinct queue metrics. This exhaustive coverage      
  ensures complete operational visibility while maintaining consistent          
  methodology across the entire model portfolio. Architecture and               
  Configuration Management The pipeline employs centralized configuration       
  management through a hierarchical dictionary structure that serves as a       
  single source of truth for all operational parameters. This configuration     
  encompasses database connections, aggregation specifications, metric          
  definitions, and Prophet hyperparameters: Config Block PROPHET_CONFIG = {     
  'database': { 'metrics_table':                                                
  f'{env}.support_core_metrics.agg_daily_queue_metrics', 'events_table':        
  f'{env}.support_core_metrics.external_events', 'bu_forecasts_table':          
  f'{env}.support_core_metrics.queue_metrics_forecast' }, 'aggregation_specs':  
  [ {'type': 'business_unit', 'model_suffix': 'total'}, {'type':                
  'business_unit_chat', 'model_suffix': 'chat'}, {'type':                       
  'business_unit_nonchat', 'model_suffix': 'non_chat'} ], 'business_units':     
  ['Cloud SMB', 'Cloud Enterprise', 'Strategic Accounts'], 'metrics': {         
  'assigned_tickets': 'assigned_tickets.total', 'created_tickets':              
  'created_tickets.total', # ... 12 additional metrics }, 'prophet_params': {   
  'interval_width': 0.80, 'forecast_horizon': 30, 'changepoint_prior_scale':    
  0.05, 'seasonality_prior_scale': 10, # ... additional parameters } } This     
  configuration maintains metric-specific parameters that allow fine-tuned      
  control over Prophet's behavior for different metric categories, recognizing  
  that stable state metrics require different modeling approaches than          
  high-volume transactional metrics. Run as of Date # Set Run as Date For       
  reproducibility RUN_AS_OF_DATE =                                              
  pd.to_datetime(os.environ.get('RUN_AS_OF_DATE',                               
  pd.Timestamp.today().normalize())) FORECAST_CONFIG['run_as_of_date'] =        
  RUN_AS_OF_DATE It is also prudent to include a Run as of and leverage this    
  to make the notebook reproducible. This limits queries, forecasts, etc, so a  
  run can be recreated as of a certain date. Advanced Modeling Features and     
  Metric-Specific Tuning The implementation recognizes that different metrics   
  exhibit fundamentally different behavioral patterns and adjusts modeling      
  parameters accordingly. Stable state metrics receive stronger seasonality     
  priors and more conservative changepoint sensitivity: Metric Config           
  'metric_specific_params': { # STABLE METRICS: Stronger seasonality, more      
  stable patterns 'any_active_tickets': { 'seasonality_prior_scale': 25, # vs   
  10 default 'changepoint_prior_scale': 0.02, # vs 0.05 default                 
  'daily_seasonality': 10, }, 'end_of_day_active_tickets': {                    
  'seasonality_prior_scale': 25, 'changepoint_prior_scale': 0.02,               
  'daily_seasonality': 10, }, # LOWER-VOLUME METRICS: More conservative         
  'reopened_tickets': { 'seasonality_prior_scale': 5,                           
  'changepoint_prior_scale': 0.03, } } The system implements conditional        
  seasonality patterns that separately model weekday and weekend behavior,      
  capturing the fundamental operational reality that support patterns differ    
  dramatically between business and weekend days: Weekday/Weekend Config # Add  
  weekend indicators prophet_df['is_weekend'] = prophet_df['ds'].dt.dayofweek   
  >= 5 prophet_df['is_weekday'] = ~prophet_df['is_weekend'] # Initialize        
  Prophet with base parameters model = Prophet(**prophet_kwargs) # Add          
  conditional seasonalities (replaces default weekly)                           
  model.add_seasonality(name='weekly_weekday', period=7, fourier_order=3,       
  condition_name='is_weekday') model.add_seasonality(name='weekly_weekend',     
  period=7, fourier_order=2, condition_name='is_weekend') # Add business cycle  
  seasonalities model.add_seasonality(name='monthly', period=30.5,              
  fourier_order=5) model.add_seasonality(name='quarterly', period=91.25,        
  fourier_order=5) Flexible Event Handling Strategy The pipeline implements an  
  intelligent multi-tier event handling system with graceful fallback           
  mechanisms. The configuration specifies the event strategy: Event Config      
  'events_config': { 'use_external_events': False,                              
  'fallback_to_pandas_holidays': True, 'include_company_events': True,          
  'holiday_calendars': [ 'USFederalHolidayCalendar',                            
  'AustraliaHolidayCalendar', 'IndiaHolidayCalendar', 'CanadaHolidayCalendar',  
  'UKBankHolidayCalendar', ], 'company_events': [ {'name': 'Fiscal Year End',   
  'month': 6, 'day': 30}, {'name': 'Fiscal Q1 End', 'month': 9, 'day': 30}, #   
  ... additional company events ] } The event loading logic implements the      
  fallback strategy and converts events to Prophet's holiday format with        
  appropriate temporal windows: External Config Block def                       
  load_external_events(config, start_date, end_date): """Load external events   
  with fallback logic""" events_df = pd.DataFrame() # Step 1: Try external      
  events table if configured if                                                 
  config['events_config']['use_external_events']: if                            
  check_events_table_exists(config['database']['events_table']): events_df =    
  spark.sql(query).toPandas() events_source = 'external_table' # Step 2:        
  Fallback to pandas holidays if needed if events_df.empty and                  
  config['events_config']['fallback_to_pandas_holidays']: events_df =           
  create_pandas_holidays(config, start_date, end_date) events_source =          
  'pandas_holidays' # Step 3: Add company events if                             
  config['events_config']['include_company_events']: company_events_df =        
  create_company_events(config, start_date, end_date) events_df =               
  pd.concat([events_df, company_events_df], ignore_index=True) return           
  events_df, events_source Each event is transformed into Prophet's holiday     
  format with impact windows: Holiday Block def prepare_holidays_df(self,       
  events_df, aggregation_name=None): """Convert events to Prophet holiday       
  format""" holidays = [] for , event in events_df.iterrows(): # Determine      
  holiday window from event metadata lower_window =                             
  -int(event.get('impact_ramp_days', 0)) upper_window =                         
  int(event.get('impact_decay_days', 1)) # Prophet limits windows to [-30, 30]  
  lower_window = max(-30, lower_window) upper_window = min(30, upper_window)    
  holiday_entry = { 'holiday': event['event_name'], 'ds': event['event_date'],  
  'lower_window': lower_window, 'upper_window': upper_window }                  
  holidays.append(holiday_entry) return pd.DataFrame(holidays) Dual-Phase       
  Training with Comprehensive Evaluation The pipeline executes a dual-phase     
  training strategy that balances rigorous evaluation with production           
  readiness. Dual Phase means have some holdout for evaluation purposes, but    
  then train on the entire dataset: Training Block def                          
  train_all_with_evaluation(self, full_data, events_df): """Train models for    
  all aggregations with evaluation""" # Split data for evaluation holdout_days  
  = self.config['prophet_params']['evaluation_holdout_days'] train_end =        
  full_data['date'].max() - timedelta(days=holdout_days) train_data =           
  full_data[full_data['date'] <= train_end] test_data =                         
  full_data[full_data['date'] > train_end] logger.info("PHASE 1: Training for   
  EVALUATION") # Train evaluation models and compute metrics for                
  aggregation_name, agg_type, model_suffix, metric in tasks: model =            
  self.train_single_model(aggregation_name, agg_type, metric, train_data,       
  train_events) if model: key = f"{aggregation_name}{model_suffix}{metric}"     
  self.evaluation_models[key] = model self.evaluate_model(key, model,           
  test_data, metric) logger.info("PHASE 2: Training for PRODUCTION") # Retrain  
  on full dataset for production for aggregation_name, agg_type, model_suffix,  
  metric in tasks: model = self.train_single_model(aggregation_name, agg_type,  
  metric, full_data, events_df) if model: key =                                 
  f"{aggregation_name}{model_suffix}{metric}" self.production_models[key] =     
  model The evaluation framework computes comprehensive performance metrics     
  including weekday/weekend segmentation and weekly breakdowns: Evaluation      
  Framework def evaluate_model(self, model_key, model, test_data, metric):      
  """Evaluate model and store metrics""" # Generate forecast and merge with     
  actuals forecast = model.predict(actual[['ds', 'is_weekend', 'is_weekday']])  
  eval_df = actual.merge(forecast[['ds', 'yhat']], on='ds', how='inner') #      
  Overall metrics with sMAPE overall_smape =                                    
  symmetric_mean_absolute_percentage_error( eval_df['y'], eval_df['yhat'] )     
  overall_metrics = { 'overall_mae': mean_absolute_error(eval_df['y'],          
  eval_df['yhat']), 'overall_smape': overall_smape,                             
  'overall_smape_assessment': assess_smape_performance(overall_smape), # ...    
  additional metrics } # Weekday/weekend segmentation eval_df['is_weekend'] =   
  eval_df['ds'].dt.dayofweek >= 5 weekday_data =                                
  eval_df[~eval_df['is_weekend']] weekend_data =                                
  eval_df[eval_df['is_weekend']] if len(weekday_data) > 0: weekday_smape =      
  symmetric_mean_absolute_percentage_error( weekday_data['y'],                  
  weekday_data['yhat'] ) overall_metrics['overall_weekday_smape'] =             
  weekday_smape overall_metrics['overall_weekday_smape_assessment'] = \         
  assess_smape_performance(weekday_smape) # Weekly performance breakdown for    
  week in range(1, 5): week_data = eval_df[(eval_df['days_from_start'] >=       
  (week-1)7) & (eval_df['days_from_start'] < week7)] if len(week_data) > 0:     
  week_smape = symmetric_mean_absolute_percentage_error( week_data['y'],        
  week_data['yhat'] ) overall_metrics[f'week{week}smape'] = week_smape          
  overall_metrics[f'week{week}smape_assessment'] = \                            
  assess_smape_performance(week_smape) Comprehensive MLflow Integration and     
  Experiment Tracking Every aspect of the pipeline is meticulously tracked      
  through MLflow within a single run context that captures the complete         
  training session: MLFlow Integration with                                     
  mlflow.start_run(run_name=f"Prophet_MultiAgg{pd.Timestamp.now().strftime('%Y  
  %m%d_%H%M')}"): # Train models prophet_pipeline =                             
  ProphetBUPipeline(PROPHET_CONFIG, events_source)                              
  prophet_pipeline.train_all_with_evaluation(bu_data, events_df) # Generate     
  forecasts bu_forecasts =                                                      
  prophet_pipeline.generate_bu_forecasts(horizon_days=30) # Log configuration   
  parameters (flattened for MLflow compatibility) config_params = {             
  'run_timestamp': pd.Timestamp.now().strftime('%Y%m%d_%H%M%S'),                
  'total_models': len(prophet_pipeline.production_models), 'n_business_units':  
  len(PROPHET_CONFIG['business_units']), 'n_metrics':                           
  len(PROPHET_CONFIG['metrics']), # ... 100+ additional parameters }            
  mlflow.log_params(config_params) # Log evaluation metrics avg_overall_smape   
  = eval_df['overall_smape'].mean() mlflow.log_metric('avg_overall_smape',      
  avg_overall_smape) mlflow.set_tag('avg_overall_smape_assessment',             
  assess_smape_performance(avg_overall_smape)) # Log metrics by aggregation     
  type for agg_type in eval_df['aggregation_type'].unique(): agg_metrics =      
  eval_df[eval_df['aggregation_type'] == agg_type] suffix =                     
  AGG_TYPE_TO_MODEL_SUFFIX[agg_type] agg_smape =                                
  agg_metrics['overall_smape'].mean()                                           
