─── Response ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Key findings (concise summary):


 1 Five Pillars to Encode in the Skill
    • A. Symphony 3-Tier Autonomy — deterministic classify_action() → 🟢 Tier
      1 (autonomous, reversible reads/safety actions/audit-trail writes), 🟡
      Tier 2 (propose-then-act with Slack ack + SLA watchdog), 🔴 Tier 3
      (human-required for prod promotion, hotfix merges, customer comms,
      policy changes). Algorithm: read→T1; reversible? blast radius? policy
      assertion? Default to higher tier when ambiguous.
    • B. ORBIT Read/Write Contract — purpose=AGENT_PLAN|AGENT_EXECUTE,
      budgeted context packs (inner/near/outer), orbit_bind_context per
      session, write-back of lineage (model/dataset/experiment/eval/deploy
      IDs) and outcome enrichment (decision, tier, policy_hits, metrics_delta)
      after every action. Bootstrap sequence required at first model
      registration.
    • C. Policy-Pack Compliance — versioned YAML rules from Policy for AI
      Agents in SDLC, Agent Budget Guardrails, Agentic Operations in the New
      SDLC, RFC-002. evaluate_policy() returns {effect, hits, 
      missing_evidence}. Per-skill enforcement points table.
    • D. Audit Emission — canonical event schema
      (actor/action/tier/rationale/policy_pack_version/policy_hits/approval/ev
      idence/outcome/orbit_pack_id/trace_id); two sinks (ORBIT outcome stream
      + Atlassian StreamHub audit log).
    • E. RFC-002 L1–L5 Maturity — full rubric with gating criteria per level;
      gate_action() callable; advancement workflow (evidence collection →
      self-check → Tier-2 propose → reviewer ack → audit emit → ORBIT/Compass
      update); auto-demotion on invariant breach.
 2 MCP Coverage — atlassian/slack/bitbucket/socrates/google_drive/gmail cover
   Jira agent-governance issues, Confluence evidence pages + audit fallback,
   Slack Tier-2 ack workflows + scheduled digests, Bitbucket rollback PRs with
   audit metadata, Socrates SQL against audit-mirror tables, Drive archival,
   Gmail formal records. All governance Jira issues anchor to user's top
   project CTSC / parent CTSC-39558.
 3 Gaps Requiring Subtask 2 Tools
    • orbit_context_client (bootstrap/read/bind/append outcome — ORBIT API not
      yet GA per worker_24)
    • policy_evaluator (or extension of orbit_context_client) —
      machine-evaluable policy packs; fail-safe DENY until built
    • audit_log_emitter (StreamHub producer; gated on ENT-3675 for
      actor_is_agent attribution)
    • pir_drafter, circuit_breaker_control, ml_lens_query_client,
      signalfx_detector_iac for L3/L4 invariant validation
 4 Integration Pattern — Skill is passive; every other MLE skill
   (drift_triggered_retraining, ml_incident_response, ml_evaluation_protocol,
   ml_launch_review_gate, cost_and_capacity_stewardship,
   ml_observability_and_slo, mle_stakeholder_reporting) wraps its workflow
   with session_start → guard(action) → execute → session_end calls to
   governance.
 5 Known Issues / Edge Cases — ENT-3675 blocks agent audit attribution; ORBIT
   MVP requires TwG fallback; Slack has no blocking ack primitive (poll +
   watchdog); slack.create_scheduled_message can't be cancelled; cannot create
   Slack channels via MCP (use pre-provisioned
   #mle-governance/#mle-launch-review); fail-safe DENY when policy evaluator
   unavailable; classifier and policy versions pinned per session for
   deterministic replay; cross-tenant isolation enforced by AGENT-POL-TEN-001.
 6 Autonomy Profile — Governance is itself fully autonomous for
   reads/classification/Jira/Slack info posts (Tier 1); auto-demotion is Tier
   2; auto-promotion and policy-pack modification are Tier 3.
