  "children/worker_1/children/worker_9/budget_guardrails.txt:\n\n````text\n     
  0  Summary This proposal defines a new capability for governing AI spend and  
  operational risk at the agent and workflow level . Atlassian already has      
  strong foundations for AI telemetry, tracing, and rate limiting through AI    
  Gateway, Arize, and related internal initiatives. What is still missing is a  
  practical control layer that can answer a simpler question: when should an    
  AI workflow warn, slow down, or stop because it is becoming too expensive or  
  behaving abnormally? This proposal introduces Agent Budget Guardrails : a     
  policy and enforcement layer that applies session-level budgets, anomaly      
  detection, operator controls, and kill-switch behaviour across AI agents and  
  long-running AI workflows. The proposal does not replace AI Gateway,          
  duplicate Overclock, or recreate ContextShield. Instead, it builds on top of  
  existing telemetry and integrates with both central and local enforcement     
  points. The problem AI agents do not fail in the same way as traditional      
  backend workloads. They can accumulate cost gradually through retries,        
  recursive tool calls, prompt amplification, long sessions, and loops that     
  are individually valid but collectively wasteful. Existing infrastructure     
  controls are necessary, but they are not sufficient for governing agent       
  behaviour. Today, Atlassian has: AI Gateway rate limits such as per-use-case  
  TPM controls Per-user abuse thresholds Rich per-call token and cost           
  telemetry Tracing and observability systems for model calls and agents What   
  Atlassian does not have is a budget-aware control plane for agent execution.  
  This creates three practical problems. Problem 1: infrastructure rate limits  
  are not budget controls Rate limiting protects shared systems from spikes     
  and abusive usage. It does not tell a team whether a workflow is spending     
  too much for the value it delivers. A use case can remain below TPM limits    
  while still generating unacceptable cost through long sessions, excessive     
  tool calls, or runaway retries. The AI Gateway team's own guardrails work     
  identifies this gap directly: current controls protect platform capacity,     
  but they do not prevent use cases from consuming beyond approved allocation.  
  Problem 2: there is no workflow-level stop mechanism When an agent behaves    
  unexpectedly, the current response is often operational and manual:           
  investigate dashboards, identify the source, and increase or adjust limits.   
  This is too slow for autonomous or semi-autonomous workflows that can burn    
  through significant spend in a short period. There is no standard capability  
  today for: Stopping a single expensive session without affecting all traffic  
  Applying soft and hard spend thresholds to a workflow Flagging anomalous      
  sessions before they become incidents Giving teams a reusable policy model    
  for AI cost governance Problem 3: cost data exists, but it is not actionable  
  at the decision point AI Gateway already emits detailed token and cost data.  
  Overclock and related efforts are building stronger reporting and             
  attribution. But attribution alone does not change behaviour. Teams need      
  controls that act during execution , not only dashboards that explain spend   
  after the fact. The missing layer is not more telemetry. The missing layer    
  is policy-backed enforcement . Why this matters now This problem is no        
  longer theoretical. Internal runbooks already show high-growth AI workloads   
  approaching token limits and requiring manual intervention. Multiple          
  internal proposals describe budgeting, anomaly detection, and guardrails as   
  required future capabilities. (see also:                                      
  https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GWP/pages/6808547672 ,                
  https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/TRUSTORG/pages/6810965399 ) Agentic   
  workflows increase the risk of nonlinear spend because one user action can    
  trigger many downstream model calls and tool invocations. Agent adoption at   
  Atlassian is accelerating. More teams are building multi-step, tool-using,    
  and autonomous AI workflows (Rovo Agents, GWP, ai-mate-automations). As       
  these patterns scale, spend becomes nonlinear: a single user action can       
  trigger chains of model calls, tool invocations, and retries that compound    
  quickly. This trajectory makes the gap between infrastructure rate limits     
  and budget governance wider over time, not narrower. Without a standard       
  guardrail capability, each team will either operate without budget controls   
  or build one-off solutions with inconsistent semantics and poor reuse. What   
  already exists Atlassian already has strong building blocks. This proposal    
  intentionally uses them rather than replacing them. Existing system What it   
  provides Why it is not enough on its own AI Gateway Per-call token and cost   
  telemetry, TPM rate limiting, abuse prevention, central traffic governance    
  Focuses on platform protection, not workflow-level budgeting or session       
  stops Arize AX LLM tracing, observability, span-level diagnostics Explains    
  behaviour but does not enforce spend policy Overclock / AI Telemetry          
  Attribution, reporting, dashboards, spend analysis Provides visibility, not   
  real-time control or stop behaviour ContextShield Local enforcement point     
  for IDE AI traffic, prompt/response scanning Focuses on security and          
  governance for local tools, not shared budget policy for agents Guard /       
  Responsible AI Content moderation and prompt DLP Addresses safety and data    
  protection, not agent spend governance The opportunity is to create a         
  capability that sits above telemetry and below operations : a runtime budget  
  policy layer. What this proposal introduces Agent Budget Guardrails Agent     
  Budget Guardrails is a reusable capability that evaluates AI workflows        
  against budget and behavioural policy, then takes action when thresholds are  
  crossed. It is intended for: Background agents Long-running workflows         
  Multi-step tool-using agents Interactive developer agents where spend can     
  accumulate over a session The core model is simple: Collect usage and cost    
  events for a workflow or session Continuously evaluate those events against   
  budget policy Warn, annotate, slow, or stop execution when policy conditions  
  are met Core capabilities 1. Session-level budgets Each workflow or session   
  can be assigned a budget envelope with policy-defined limits such as:         
  Maximum token budget Maximum dollar budget Maximum tool calls Maximum model   
  invocations Maximum execution duration This allows teams to govern an         
  individual workflow rather than only a service-wide traffic class. 2. Soft    
  thresholds and warnings Before hard stops are applied, the system can emit    
  warnings at policy-defined thresholds such as 50%, 75%, and 90% of budget.    
  Warnings can be surfaced to: Developers in the IDE or CLI Operators through   
  internal notifications Service owners through telemetry and audit channels    
  This enables intervention before spend becomes waste. 3. Hard stops and kill  
  switches When a session exceeds a hard limit, the system can terminate or     
  block further execution for that workflow while leaving unrelated traffic     
  unaffected. This is materially different from broad TPM throttling. It        
  allows precise control over the session that is causing cost risk. Examples:  
  Stop a background code-fix agent after repeated low-value retries Block an    
  autonomous workflow after excessive tool-call recursion Terminate a session   
  that has exceeded its approved budget envelope 4. Anomaly detection Not all   
  problematic sessions exceed static thresholds. Some are abnormal relative to  
  expected behaviour. Agent Budget Guardrails should detect patterns such as:   
  Session cost far above historical median for the same workflow Sudden         
  increase in tool calls per task Large divergence in token consumption for     
  similar prompts Repeated retries with minimal useful output This creates a    
  second line of defense beyond fixed budgets. 5. Policy-driven defaults Teams  
  should not have to invent their own spend governance semantics. The system    
  should support centrally managed defaults such as: Per-use-case default       
  session budgets Per-team maximum allowed budget ranges Model-specific         
  constraints Tool-call ceilings for autonomous flows Local owners can set      
  lower limits, but not exceed organization-approved bounds without explicit    
  override. 6. Unified cost event model The capability should consume a         
  standard cost event shape regardless of where the traffic originates. That    
  allows: Backend and product AI traffic to supply events from AI Gateway       
  telemetry Local IDE tooling to supply events from ContextShield when          
  applicable A single policy engine to evaluate both central and local          
  workflows consistently How it works Execution model Agent Budget Guardrails   
  is best understood as a policy evaluation and enforcement layer , not as a    
  new AI proxy. The capability has three parts: Event ingestion : usage,        
  token, cost, tool-call, and session metadata are emitted from existing        
  systems Policy evaluation : a budget engine continuously evaluates the        
  current session state against configured rules Enforcement action :           
  warnings, annotations, throttles, or stops are issued when policy conditions  
  are met Policy inputs Each evaluation uses some combination of: Current       
  cumulative tokens Current cumulative cost Current tool-call count Execution   
  time Workflow type or use case Model family Team or owner metadata            
  Historical baselines for anomaly comparison Enforcement points The proposal   
  supports two main enforcement points: Enforcement point Applies to Role AI    
  Gateway-side integration Backend and product AI traffic Uses central          
  telemetry and existing control surfaces to enforce workflow budget policy     
  ContextShield-side integration Local IDE and CLI traffic Applies the same     
  budget policy to locally mediated AI sessions and emits compatible events     
  This ensures the capability is reusable across both centrally routed and      
  locally mediated AI usage. What makes this different from existing work Not   
  a dashboard project This proposal does not aim to build a new generic         
  analytics or reporting product. Reporting already has strong momentum         
  through Overclock, AI Cost Attribution, and existing AI Gateway telemetry.    
  The value here is real-time control, not retrospective reporting. Not a       
  replacement for AI Gateway AI Gateway remains the central execution and       
  telemetry layer for backend and product AI traffic. Agent Budget Guardrails   
  extends its usefulness by adding budget-aware workflow policy. Not a          
  duplicate of ContextShield proposal ContextShield addresses local security    
  and governance for developer AI tools. Agent Budget Guardrails addresses      
  spend behaviour and workflow control. The two systems complement each other   
  and can share event formats and enforcement semantics. Why this is a good     
  standalone proposal This proposal has strong justification as a separate      
  body of work because it is both needed and unowned . The need is repeatedly   
  acknowledged in internal proposals, runbooks, and planning documents No       
  system today provides session-level AI budget enforcement The capability      
  builds on top of existing telemetry rather than requiring a new foundational  
  platform It complements, rather than competes with, AI Gateway, Overclock,    
  Arize, and ContextShield It also has a clean decision boundary: visibility    
  tells you what happened, while budget guardrails determine what should        
  happen next. Expected benefits Reduce waste from runaway or low-value agent   
  sessions Move from manual intervention to policy-backed control Give teams a  
  reusable governance model instead of one-off local logic Improve confidence   
  in adopting more autonomous AI workflows Create a shared control plane that   
  works across both central and local AI execution paths Scope boundaries This  
  proposal is intentionally narrow. It does not include: Building a new LLM     
  observability platform Replacing AI Gateway telemetry or rate limiting        
  Building a generic spend dashboard from scratch Re-proposing ContextShield's  
  security scanning functionality Solving every dimension of AI governance in   
  one system Open questions How much of AI Gateway's current rate-limit and     
  abuse-prevention machinery can be extended rather than duplicated? What       
  should be the default unit of policy: session, workflow, engineer, team, or   
  use case? Which enforcement action should be default at first: warn,          
  annotate, throttle, or stop? How should anomaly baselines be computed to      
  avoid alert fatigue? How should local-tool events from ContextShield be       
  normalised with AI Gateway telemetry? What operator experience is needed for  
  overrides, approvals, and emergency stops? \n````",                           
  'children/worker_1/children/worker_9/migration_guide.txt:\n\n````text\n   0   
  Purpose This page is a single starting point for teams migrating existing     
  SageMaker-hosted models to TeamServe. It combines an example of the “QI       
  migration framework” with broader TeamServe onboarding guidance, practical    
  validation advice, and rollout guardrails for service owners and model        
  owners. This doc is created by Rovo with reference docs below. Please leave   
  feedback / questions if you think this doc is helpful or unhelpful. Thanks!   
  What is changing SageMaker to TeamServe migration is more than an endpoint    
  swap. In most cases, teams need to move from a SageMaker-style packaging      
  model to a TeamServe-compatible serving contract, usually backed by Triton    
  or another supported TeamServe runtime. The migration has three parts:        
  Prepare and register a TeamServe-compatible model artifact Deploy that        
  artifact to TeamServe in the required environments and regions Switch client  
  traffic gradually, with monitoring and rollback Rule of thumb: The migration  
  framework Step 1: Prepare the model artifact in ML Registry v3 Owner: model   
  owner Your first job is to produce a TeamServe-compatible artifact and        
  register it in ML Registry v3. Provide a new TeamServe-compatible model       
  artifact through the v3 registry For many SageMaker models, this means        
  repackaging from an inference.py style entrypoint into a Triton-compatible    
  structure, commonly including: model.py implementing TritonPythonModel when   
  using Triton Python backend config.pbtxt describing model inputs, outputs,    
  shapes, and backend a packaged environment with the correct Python and        
  library versions Step 2: Deploy to TeamServe Owner: TeamServe team and/or     
  deployment owner, with model owner support For “QI-style” aggregator          
  migrations, deployment should cover all required prod aggregator regions ,    
  not partial coverage. The reference inventory listed these regions:           
  prod-west2 prod-east prod-euwest prod-euwest2 prod-eucentral prod-apse2       
  prod-apne prod-apne2 prod-apsouth Deployment outputs should include: the      
  TeamServe endpoint URL Expected outcome: the model is deployed in TeamServe   
  with endpoint details ready for integration, and infra is sized from real     
  traffic rather than guesswork. Step 3: Switch client traffic safely Owner:    
  service owner / calling service team Step 3 — Aggregator-side rewrite +       
  cutover (suggested expansion) Build a dedicated TeamServeClient per model     
  (or per model family). Don't try to overload the existing                     
  SageMakerInferenceClient — the request/response contracts, auth, timeouts,    
  and retry semantics are different enough that a clean parallel client is      
  safer. Concretely: BertQueryClassifierTeamServeClient ,                       
  DocTypeIntentTeamServeClient , QueryEnricherTeamServeClient ,                 
  NavBoostPrecisionTeamServeClient , etc. Re-implement inference logic per      
  model. For each model the migration involves more than just swapping the      
  endpoint URL — input encoding (tokenization, padding, batching), output       
  decoding (logits → label, dequantization, etc.), and any inference.py         
  pre/post-processing that used to live on the SageMaker side need an explicit  
  home. Spell out for each model whether that logic moves into the Triton       
  artifact or into the new TeamServeClient . Wire it behind a Statsig flag via  
  AggregatorFeatureService.isEnabled(accountId, Optional) — fallback-friendly   
  signature unless strict tenant gating is required. Suggest a consistent       
  naming convention, e.g. FF_QI_TEAMSERVE_SHADOW + FF_QITEAMSERVE_CUTOVER .     
  Three-phase rollout per model , not a single "ramp": Shadow: call both        
  SageMaker and TeamServe in parallel, return SageMaker, log payload + latency  
  diff. Run for at least N days with measurable diff threshold (e.g. <1%        
  semantic divergence) before progressing. Canary: 1% → 5% → 25% → 50% → 100%,  
  starting in one prod region (typically prod-west2 ), each step soaking ≥24h.  
  Cutover: flip default in all regions; SageMaker endpoint and config retained  
  for ≥2 weeks of stable 100% TeamServe traffic before any decommission work.   
  Monitoring contract. Mirror the existing sagemaker.inference.sli.v2           
  histogram with a teamserve.inference.sli equivalent (same tags, same          
  buckets) so SageMaker-vs-TeamServe parity can be diffed in a single SignalFx  
  chart. Track p50/p95/p99 latency, error rate, timeout rate, and downstream    
  relevance signals (CTR, NDCG where applicable). Rollback path = flip the      
  flag back. Document that this must be a no-deploy operation — and explicitly  
  call out that config and SageMaker endpoint must not be removed during ramp.  
  Decommission checklist (this is missing entirely today): post-soak, remove    
  the SM_ENDPOINT* env vars, the sageMaker.models entries in                    
  application-prod-*.yml , the old client code path, and the now-unused         
  Statsig flags (per workspace conventions — including RatchetsTest exclusion   
  list). Recommended end-to-end workflow for new teams Phase 1: Assess the      
  existing SageMaker model Document current endpoint name and callers Capture   
  request and response schema Capture dependencies from inference.py request    
  rate payload sizes batching behavior latency SLOs Identify whether the model  
  uses custom Python logic, ONNX, PyTorch, or another runtime Phase 2:          
  Repackage for TeamServe Convert SageMaker packaging to a TeamServe-supported  
  contract Prefer the simplest serving backend that fits the model Define       
  explicit input/output contracts in artifact config Package the exact runtime  
  dependencies needed for inference Common packaging translation SageMaker:     
  inference.py + serialized weights Typical TeamServe Triton target: model.py   
  + config.pbtxt + model version directory + packaged dependencies If your      
  model has minimal custom logic and exports cleanly, ONNX may be worth         
  evaluating, but it should be validated empirically rather than assumed to be  
  faster. Phase 3: Register and validate in ML Registry If not done already,    
  upload the model artifact to ML Registry Use v3 registry workflows If         
  needed, migrate older model metadata using ml_registry_migrate_model Phase    
  4: Validate in Launchpad Before production deployment, validate behavior on   
  TeamServe Launchpad. Launch the model & verify the model loads successfully   
  Review logs and metrics Delete the endpoint when done Phase 5: Load test      
  before production sizing Use Launchpad load testing to estimate stable        
  throughput and infer cost. This is especially important for GPU-backed        
  services. Use synthetic but representative production-like traffic Observe    
  where latency and errors begin rising Determine an operating point with       
  stability headroom Use that data to choose instance sizing and autoscaling    
  settings Phase 6: Deploy to staging then prod Promote to staging with the     
  production-like config path Run integration tests from the real calling       
  service Deploy to all required prod regions Share endpoint URLs and config    
  contract with the service owner Phase 7: Cut over gradually Compare           
  TeamServe vs SageMaker metrics during ramp Retain immediate rollback ability  
  QI inventory example The QI migration inventory is a strong example of how    
  to make scope explicit for a service migration. The referenced page           
  identified these SageMaker-backed models in xpsearch-aggregator: Model name   
  Environment variable / endpoint key Aggregator client bert-query-classifier   
  SM_ENDPOINT_BERT_QUERY_CLASSIFIER_V0_ENDPOINT_NAME BertQueryClassifier /      
  QueryClassifierClientImpl doc-type-query-intent                               
  SM_ENDPOINT_DOC_TYPE_QUERY_INTENT_ENDPOINT_NAME                               
  ModelBasedDocTypeIntentDetectionClient / DocIntentDetectorService             
  structured-query-classifier / structured-query-classifier-updated             
  SM_ENDPOINT_QU_CLASSIFIER_V0_ENDPOINT_NAME /                                  
  SM_ENDPOINT_QU_CLASSIFIER_UPDATED_V0_ENDPOINT_NAME                            
  QueryUnderstandingClassifierClient / StructuredQueryTranslationServiceImpl /  
  GlobalFilterTranslator query-enricher-model                                   
  SM_ENDPOINT_QUERY_ENRICHER_V1_ENDPOINT_NAME QueryEmbeddingClient              
  navboost-precision-model SM_ENDPOINT_NAVBOOST_PRECISION_V1_ENDPOINT_NAME      
  NavBoostPrecisionScoringClient Suggestion for your master doc: add a living   
  inventory section per org or service with columns for model owner, service    
  owner, TeamServe readiness, baseline metrics, rollout status, and rollback    
  status. Migration checklist Identify all SageMaker models, endpoint names,    
  and calling services Repackage the model into a TeamServe-supported artifact  
  Register the artifact in ML Registry v3 Validate launch and inference in      
  Launchpad Deploy to required TeamServe environments and regions Ramp traffic  
  gradually while monitoring latency, errors, and relevance Remove old          
  SageMaker endpoints only after confirming no remaining consumers Frequently   
  asked questions Do I need to move to v3? Yes. If your current model lineage   
  is still tied to older registry patterns, use the ml_registry_migrate_model   
  module in ML Studio to help move the model into the v3 registry workflow.     
  The module is here . Do I always need to convert the model to Triton format?  
  Yes. Should I use ONNX for better performance? Maybe, but do not assume it    
  is automatically better. If your model can export cleanly and does not        
  depend on heavy custom Python logic, ONNX can be worth testing. Performance   
  should be validated with representative requests rather than chosen on        
  theory alone. When should service owners get involved? Service owners should  
  be involved no later than pre-production validation. They own client          
  integration, endpoint wiring, feature flags, rollout strategy, and success    
  metrics after the model is callable in TeamServe. What should I keep from     
  the old SageMaker setup during migration? Keep the old SageMaker endpoint,    
  config path, and rollback mechanism until the TeamServe endpoint has carried  
  full production traffic stably for the agreed soak period. References QI      
  SageMaker → TeamServe Migration Plan & Inventory Compiled guide of            
  onboarding Sagemaker models to TEAMServe ⇒ main phases Launchpad on           
  TEAMServe User Manual Onboarding a Model to TeamServe ML Registry migrate     
  model module \n````',                                                         
  'children/worker_1/children/worker_9/teamserve_costs.txt:\n\n````text\n   0   
  Teamserve Cost Savings vs SageMaker (replica-for-replica) One number          
  Teamserve saves ~$5.6M/year (≈59% cheaper) vs running the same replicas on    
  SageMaker. Workload compared: 33 models , 615 replicas , 10 regions . The     
  math All costs are effective (i.e., after typical discounts), projected       
  using 730 hours/month . Metric Teamserve (effective) SageMaker (estimated     
  effective) Delta Monthly $320,320 $788,459 -$468,139 Annual $3,843,842        
  $9,461,507 -$5,617,665 Percent — — ~59% reduction What drives it Top 3        
  savings drivers (monthly): 1) p5en.48xlarge (GPU) Teamserve runs 16 replicas  
  of p5en.48xlarge . Teamserve: $67,791/month SageMaker list price:             
  $47.8538/hr Estimated SageMaker effective hourly: $23.52/hr (see              
  methodology) SageMaker estimated effective monthly: $274,759/month So         
  p5en.48xlarge is about: $206,968/month savings $2,483,610/year savings 2)     
  g5.2xlarge Teamserve runs 222 replicas of g5.2xlarge . Teamserve:             
  $85,522/month SageMaker estimated effective monthly: $225,383/month So        
  g5.2xlarge is about: $139,861/month savings $1,678,336/year savings 3)        
  g6e.xlarge Teamserve runs 334 replicas of g6e.xlarge . Teamserve:             
  $141,470/month SageMaker estimated effective monthly: $194,964/month So       
  g6e.xlarge is about: $53,494/month savings $641,930/year savings p5en         
  dominates, but the fleet savings add up across the next tiers too. How we     
  calculated this Teamserve monthly cost Source:                                
  operations/terraform/model-owners-for-signalfx.json We used:                  
  metadata.total_monthly_projected_effective_cost for total Teamserve monthly   
  metadata.cost_by_instance_type_effective["p5en.48xlarge"] for the p5en slice  
  SageMaker monthly cost (replica-for-replica) Source: sagemaker_cost.csv       
  (exported from Databricks query) For each instance type that appears in our   
  deployments, we used SageMaker effective hourly rates from the CSV and        
  computed: monthly_cost = hourly_effective_cost × replicas × 730 Handling      
  p5en.48xlarge (only list price available) We only had list pricing for        
  ml.p5en.48xlarge , not effective. So we estimated effective price by using    
  the global discount ratio observed in sagemaker_cost.csv : For                
