  Studio user guides ML Studio developer site\n````",                           
  'artifacts/ml_fabric_alpha.txt:\n\n````text\n   0 Welcome We are evaluating   
  a new ML orchestration and experimentation stack on the ML Fabric dev         
  cluster. This page is your guide to accessing, using, and providing feedback  
  on the platform. Everything described here is live and ready for hands-on     
  testing. No local installation is required beyond VPN access and kubectl .    
  Cluster: mlp-compute-dev-usw2-dhbm Branch: argo-workflows-demo in             
  mlp-kube-argocd Status: Alpha — validated end-to-end including GPU workflows  
  on May 11, 2026 Platform at a Glance Service Purpose Status URL Argo          
  Workflows Workflow orchestration — DAGs, GPU scheduling, gating Live          
  argo.dhbm.kitt-inf.net JupyterHub Managed notebook servers — interactive      
  development Live jupyterhub.dhbm.kitt-inf.net MLflow Experiment tracking,     
  metrics, model registry Live mlflow.dhbm.kitt-inf.net Kubeflow Pipelines      
  Python-native ML pipeline SDK and UI Port-forward only kubectl port-forward   
  svc/ml-pipeline-ui -n kubeflow 8080:80 All services are accessible over VPN.  
  No port-forwarding is needed for Argo, JupyterHub, or MLflow. 1. Argo         
  Workflows — Orchestration What it does Argo Workflows is the orchestration    
  engine. It runs directed acyclic graphs (DAGs) of containerized steps on      
  Kubernetes, with native support for GPU scheduling, artifact passing,         
  conditional execution, and retry logic. Think of it as the engine that        
  coordinates multi-step ML jobs — quantization, fine-tuning, evaluation,       
  model registration — as a single auditable workflow. How to access Open the   
  Argo UI in your browser: https://argo.dhbm.kitt-inf.net No credentials        
  required. You will see all active and completed workflows, their DAG          
  structure, step logs, durations, and artifacts. How to interact Option A:     
  Submit from the UI Click Workflow Templates in the left sidebar Select a      
  template (e.g. quantization-recipe-template ) Click Submit Adjust parameters  
  if desired, then click Submit Watch the DAG execute in real time Option B:    
  Submit from the command line # Apply the latest templates kubectl apply -k    
  kube-config/dev/dhbm/argo-workflows-demo # Submit a quantization run kubectl  
  create -f                                                                     
  kube-config/kustomize/argo-workflows-demo/recipes/quantization-example-submi  
  t.yaml # Watch progress kubectl get wf -n argo -w Option C: Submit from a     
  JupyterHub notebook from kubernetes import client, config                     
  config.load_incluster_config() # See the 03-end-to-end notebook for a         
  complete example Available workflow templates Template GPU What it            
  demonstrates ml-mini-pipeline-template No Synthetic data, train, evaluate,    
  conditional promotion gate gpu-quantization-template A10G GPU smoke test —    
  runs nvidia-smi quantization-recipe-template A10G Full quantization: resolve  
  model, calibrate, quantize, evaluate, register fine-tuning-recipe-template    
  A10G Full fine-tuning: resolve model, prepare data, train (LoRA/QLoRA),       
  evaluate, register distillation-recipe-template A10G Full distillation:       
  teacher logits, student training, evaluation, registration What to look for   
  Does the DAG visualization make sense for your workflows? Are the step logs   
  easy to find and read? Does the parameterization model feel right? How does   
  the GPU scheduling experience feel (Karpenter provisioning takes 2-5          
  minutes)? 2. JupyterHub — Interactive Notebooks What it does JupyterHub       
  provides managed, on-demand notebook servers running inside the cluster.      
  Each user gets an isolated JupyterLab environment with direct in-cluster      
  access to Argo, MLflow, and Kubeflow Pipelines — no port-forwarding, no       
  local setup. How to access Open https://jupyterhub.dhbm.kitt-inf.net Log in   
  with any username and password mlplatform Select a compute profile: CPU       
  Standard: 1-4 CPU, 2-8 GB RAM CPU Large: 4-8 CPU, 8-32 GB RAM Click Start     
  What you can do Interactive data exploration — pandas, numpy, scipy,          
  matplotlib are pre-installed Train models interactively — scikit-learn is     
  available; install PyTorch or TensorFlow with pip Log experiments to MLflow   
  — the tracking server is reachable in-cluster at http://mlflow.mlflow:80      
  Submit KFP pipelines — install kfp and connect to                             
  http://ml-pipeline.kubeflow:8888 Submit Argo workflows — use the Kubernetes   
  Python client to create Workflow objects Quick start from a notebook cell #   
  Log a run to MLflow import mlflow                                             
  mlflow.set_tracking_uri("http://mlflow.mlflow:80")                            
  mlflow.set_experiment("my-first-experiment") with mlflow.start_run():         
  mlflow.log_param("learning_rate", 0.01) mlflow.log_metric("accuracy", 0.95)   
  print("Logged to MLflow!") Demo notebooks The following notebooks are         
  available in the repo at kube-config/kustomize/jupyterhub/notebooks/ :        
  Notebook What it covers 01-getting-started.ipynb Environment setup,           
  connectivity checks, train a model, log to MLflow                             
  02-mlflow-experiment-tracking.ipynb Hyperparameter sweep, compare runs,       
  register best model 03-end-to-end-train-log-deploy.ipynb Submit an Argo       
  workflow from notebook, track results in MLflow Current limitations           
  Authentication: Dummy auth (any username works). Real SSO is a follow-on      
  task. Storage: User data does not persist across server restarts. Save        
  important work to S3 or git. GPU notebooks: Not yet available. GPU compute    
  is accessed through Argo workflow submissions. Custom packages: Install with  
  pip install in a cell. Packages are lost on server restart. What to look for  
  Does the notebook spawn quickly enough? Is the in-cluster connectivity to     
  MLflow and KFP seamless? What packages would you want pre-installed in the    
  default image? Would GPU notebook profiles be useful, or is GPU access        
  through Argo sufficient? 3. MLflow — Experiment Tracking and Model Registry   
  What it does MLflow tracks experiments, parameters, metrics, and artifacts    
  across all training and evaluation runs. It also provides a model registry    
  for versioning and promoting models through lifecycle stages. Every workflow  
  step — whether triggered from a notebook, an Argo DAG, or a KFP pipeline —    
  can log to the same MLflow instance. How to access Open the MLflow UI:        
  https://mlflow.dhbm.kitt-inf.net No credentials required. You will see        
  experiments, runs, metrics, parameters, and registered models. How to log     
  from code From a notebook or Python script import mlflow                      
  mlflow.set_tracking_uri("http://mlflow.mlflow:80")                            
  mlflow.set_experiment("quantization-recipes") with                            
  mlflow.start_run(run_name="int8-distilbert"): mlflow.log_param("model",       
  "distilbert-base-uncased") mlflow.log_param("method", "int8")                 
  mlflow.log_metric("original_size_mb", 268)                                    
  mlflow.log_metric("quantized_size_mb", 67)                                    
  mlflow.log_metric("accuracy_delta", -0.003) From an Argo workflow step        
  Workflow templates already include the MLflow tracking URI as a parameter (   
  mlflow-tracking-uri: http://mlflow.mlflow:80 ). Steps can log metrics         
  directly using the MLflow Python client. What to look for Does the            
  experiment/run structure make sense for your workflows? Are the logged        
  metrics and parameters useful? Would you use the model registry for           
  promotion workflows? What additional integrations would be valuable (e.g.,    
  artifact storage on S3, model serving from registry)? 4. Kubeflow Pipelines   
  — Python-Native ML Pipelines (Optional) What it does Kubeflow Pipelines       
  (KFP) provides a Python SDK for defining ML pipelines with typed components,  
  artifact tracking, and experiment management. It uses Argo Workflows as its   
  execution backend. KFP is available for teams that prefer Python-first        
  pipeline authoring over YAML. How to access KFP does not yet have an          
  external ingress. Access the UI via port-forward: kubectl port-forward        
  svc/ml-pipeline-ui -n kubeflow 8080:80 Then open http://localhost:8080 . How  
  to submit a pipeline from kfp import dsl, compiler from kfp.client import     
  Client @dsl.component(base_image="python:3.11-slim") def                      
  train(learning_rate: float) -> str: return f"Trained with                     
  lr={learning_rate}" @dsl.pipeline(name="example-pipeline") def                
  my_pipeline(lr: float = 0.01): train(learning_rate=lr)                        
  compiler.Compiler().compile(my_pipeline, "pipeline.yaml") client =            
  Client(host="http://ml-pipeline.kubeflow:8888") # from JupyterHub             
  client.create_run_from_pipeline_package("pipeline.yaml") Current status KFP   
  is running and functional but has not been as extensively tested as Argo      
  Workflows. It is included for comparison and evaluation purposes. Building    
  Argo Workflows: From Scratch and From ML Studio This section covers two       
  paths: building brand new Argo workflows, and migrating existing ML Studio    
  workflows to Argo. Concepts you need to know Argo concept What it means ML    
  Studio equivalent Workflow A single run of a DAG — created, executed,         
  completed A workflow run / job execution WorkflowTemplate A reusable,         
  parameterized workflow definition workflow_descriptor.yml Template A single   
  step definition (container, script, or resource) A task or module_task DAG    
  Directed acyclic graph of templates with dependencies depends_on              
  relationships between tasks Parameters Typed inputs/outputs passed between    
  steps parameters on tasks and variables on workflows Artifacts Files passed   
  between steps (models, datasets, reports) DBFS paths / script outputs         
  CronWorkflow A workflow that runs on a schedule Scheduled workflow runs Path  
  A: Building a new workflow from scratch Step 1: Start with a simple DAG The   
  smallest useful Argo workflow is a DAG with two steps where one depends on    
  the other: apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: Workflow metadata:          
  generateName: my-first-dag- namespace: argo spec: entrypoint: my-dag          
  templates: - name: my-dag dag: tasks: - name: step-one template: step-one -   
  name: step-two template: step-two dependencies: [step-one] - name: step-one   
  script: image: python:3.11-slim command: [python] source: | print("Step one:  
  preparing data") with open("/tmp/result.txt", "w") as f: f.write("data is     
  ready") - name: step-two script: image: python:3.11-slim command: [python]    
  source: | print("Step two: training model") print("Done!") Submit it:         
  kubectl create -f my-first-dag.yaml kubectl get wf -n argo -w Step 2: Add     
  parameters Make it configurable: spec: arguments: parameters: - name:         
  learning-rate value: "0.01" - name: epochs value: "10" entrypoint: my-dag     
  templates: - name: my-dag dag: tasks: - name: train template: train           
  arguments: parameters: - name: lr value:                                      
  "{{workflow.parameters.learning-rate}}" - name: epochs value:                 
  "{{workflow.parameters.epochs}}" - name: train inputs: parameters: - name:    
  lr - name: epochs script: image: python:3.11-slim command: [python] source:   
  | lr = float("{{inputs.parameters.lr}}") epochs =                             
  int("{{inputs.parameters.epochs}}") print(f"Training with lr={lr},            
  epochs={epochs}") Step 3: Add artifacts (pass data between steps) Step one    
  produces a file; step two consumes it: - name: generate-data script: image:   
  python:3.11-slim command: [python] source: | import json data = [{"x": i,     
  "y": i * 2} for i in range(100)] with open("/tmp/dataset.json", "w") as f:    
  json.dump(data, f) outputs: artifacts: - name: dataset path:                  
  /tmp/dataset.json - name: train-model inputs: artifacts: - name: dataset      
  path: /tmp/dataset.json script: image: python:3.11-slim command: [python]     
  source: | import json with open("/tmp/dataset.json") as f: data =             
  json.load(f) print(f"Training on {len(data)} rows") Step 4: Add a             
  conditional gate Only deploy if evaluation passes: - name: my-dag dag:        
  tasks: - name: train template: train - name: evaluate template: evaluate      
  dependencies: [train] - name: deploy template: deploy dependencies:           
  [evaluate] when: "{{tasks.evaluate.outputs.parameters.passed}} == true" -     
  name: notify-failure template: notify-failure dependencies: [evaluate] when:  
  "{{tasks.evaluate.outputs.parameters.passed}} == false" Step 5: Add GPU For   
  steps that need GPU compute: - name: train-gpu nodeSelector: nodepool: g5-2x  
  tolerations: - key: nvidia.com/gpu operator: Exists effect: NoSchedule -      
  key: atlassian.com/has-gpu operator: Equal value: "true" effect: NoSchedule   
  script: image: nvidia/cuda:12.4.1-base-ubuntu22.04 command: [bash] source: |  
  nvidia-smi echo "Training on GPU" resources: requests: nvidia.com/gpu: "1"    
  limits: nvidia.com/gpu: "1" env: - name: HF_TOKEN valueFrom: secretKeyRef:    
  name: mlp-hf-token-secret key: HF_TOKEN Step 6: Make it a reusable            
  WorkflowTemplate Convert your workflow into a template that anyone can        
  submit with different parameters: apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind:      
  WorkflowTemplate # Changed from Workflow metadata: name:                      
  my-training-pipeline # Fixed name, not generateName namespace: argo spec:     
  arguments: parameters: - name: learning-rate value: "0.01" entrypoint:        
  my-dag templates: # ... same templates as before Apply once: kubectl apply    
  -f my-training-pipeline-template.yaml Submit runs from it: apiVersion:        
  argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: Workflow metadata: generateName: my-training-run-  
  namespace: argo spec: workflowTemplateRef: name: my-training-pipeline         
  arguments: parameters: - name: learning-rate value: "0.001" Or submit         
  directly from the Argo UI: click Workflow Templates → select your template →  
  Submit. Path B: Migrating an ML Studio workflow to Argo ML Studio workflows   
  use a workflow_descriptor.yml format. Here is how each concept maps to Argo.  
  ML Studio workflow structure # ML Studio workflow_descriptor.yml name:        
  my-ml-workflow use_case: search_relevance team: search max_concurrent_runs:   
  5 realms: - us variables: - name: start_date value: "2024-02-01" tasks: -     
  name: "prepare_data" script_path: "dbfs:/ml-job-files/prepare_data.py"        
  depends_on: [] parameters: - name: "start_date" value: "{{start_date}}" -     
  name: "train_model" script_path: "/Users/jdoe/train.py" depends_on:           
  [prepare_data] parameters: - name: "epochs" value: "10" platform_override:    
  "gpu_cluster" platforms: - name: "default_cluster" is_default: true type:     
  databricks/spark instance_type: r6id.8xlarge - name: "gpu_cluster" type:      
  databricks/spark instance_type: g5.2xlarge Equivalent Argo WorkflowTemplate   
  apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: WorkflowTemplate metadata: name:       
  my-ml-workflow namespace: argo labels: team: search use-case:                 
  search-relevance spec: arguments: parameters: - name: start-date value:       
  "2024-02-01" - name: epochs value: "10" entrypoint: workflow-dag templates:   
  # DAG definition — maps ML Studio depends_on - name: workflow-dag dag:        
  tasks: - name: prepare-data template: prepare-data arguments: parameters: -   
  name: start-date value: "{{workflow.parameters.start-date}}" - name:          
  train-model template: train-model dependencies: [prepare-data] # maps to      
  depends_on: [prepare_data] arguments: parameters: - name: epochs value:       
  "{{workflow.parameters.epochs}}" # Task definitions — each ML Studio task     
  becomes a template - name: prepare-data inputs: parameters: - name:           
  start-date script: image: your-registry/prepare-data:latest # Your code,      
  containerized command: [python, /app/prepare_data.py] args: -                 
  "--start-date={{inputs.parameters.start-date}}" outputs: artifacts: - name:   
  dataset path: /tmp/output/dataset # GPU task — maps to platform_override:     
  "gpu_cluster" - name: train-model inputs: parameters: - name: epochs          
  artifacts: - name: dataset path: /tmp/input/dataset from:                     
  "{{tasks.prepare-data.outputs.artifacts.dataset}}" nodeSelector: nodepool:    
  g5-2x # maps to instance_type: g5.2xlarge tolerations: - key: nvidia.com/gpu  
  operator: Exists effect: NoSchedule - key: atlassian.com/has-gpu operator:    
  Equal value: "true" effect: NoSchedule script: image:                         
  your-registry/train-model:latest command: [python, /app/train.py] args: -     
  "--epochs={{inputs.parameters.epochs}}" resources: requests: nvidia.com/gpu:  
  "1" limits: nvidia.com/gpu: "1" env: - name: HF_TOKEN valueFrom:              
  secretKeyRef: name: mlp-hf-token-secret key: HF_TOKEN Mapping reference ML    
  Studio concept Argo equivalent Notes name metadata.name WorkflowTemplate      
  name use_case metadata.labels.use-case Label for filtering team               
  metadata.labels.team Label for filtering and RBAC variables                   
  spec.arguments.parameters Workflow-level parameters tasks[].name DAG task     
  name + template name Both the DAG reference and the template definition       
  tasks[].script_path script.image + command Python code packaged in a          
  container image tasks[].depends_on dag.tasks[].dependencies Direct mapping    
  tasks[].parameters inputs.parameters Passed via DAG task arguments            
  tasks[].platform_override nodeSelector + tolerations + resources Maps to      
  Kubernetes scheduling primitives platforms[].instance_type                    
  nodeSelector.nodepool Karpenter nodepool selection platforms[].type:          
  databricks/spark script.image or SparkApplication CRD Container for simple    
  tasks; SparkApplication for Spark jobs max_concurrent_runs parallelism in     
  workflow spec Limits concurrent steps email_notifications Exit handler        
  template Argo runs a notification step on completion/failure Scheduled runs   
  CronWorkflow Kubernetes-native cron scheduling Migration checklist for one    
  workflow [ ] Identify all tasks and their dependencies from                   
  workflow_descriptor.yml [ ] For each task, determine: is it a Python script,  
  notebook, or Spark job? [ ] Package each task's code into a container image   
  (Dockerfile + push to registry) [ ] Map depends_on to Argo DAG dependencies   
  [ ] Map parameters and variables to Argo parameters [ ] Map                   
  platform_override to nodeSelector + tolerations + resources [ ] Replace DBFS  
  paths with S3 URIs or Argo artifact passing [ ] Add MLflow logging to track   
  metrics [ ] Test the workflow on the dev cluster [ ] Compare results with     
  the Databricks version Key difference: code packaging The biggest change      
  from ML Studio to Argo is how code is packaged. In ML Studio, you point to a  
  Python script on DBFS or a Databricks path: script_path:                      
  "dbfs:/ml-job-files/prepare_data.py" In Argo, your code runs inside a         
  container image: image: your-registry/prepare-data:latest This means you      
  need to: 1. Create a Dockerfile that installs your dependencies and copies    
  your code 2. Build and push the image to a container registry 3. Reference    
  the image in the Argo template For quick prototyping, you can embed short     
  Python scripts directly in the Argo YAML using script.source (as we do in     
  the demo recipes). For production, always use a proper container image.       
  Quick prototype: embed existing script inline If your ML Studio task runs a   
  short Python script, you can embed it directly for testing: - name:           
  prepare-data script: image: python:3.11-slim command: [python] source: | #    
  Paste your existing Python code here for quick testing import pandas as pd    
  print("Preparing data...") # ... your logic ... print("Done!") This gets you  
  running quickly. Then migrate to a proper container image for production.     
  Getting Started — Your First 15 Minutes Here is the fastest path to           
  experiencing the full stack: Minutes 1-3: See an ML workflow run Open         
  https://argo.dhbm.kitt-inf.net Click Workflow Templates in the sidebar        
  Select ml-mini-pipeline-template Click Submit , then Submit again Watch the   
  DAG execute — each step turns green as it completes Minutes 4-8: Open a       
  notebook Open https://jupyterhub.dhbm.kitt-inf.net Log in (any username,      
  password: mlplatform ) Select CPU Standard, click Start Open a new Python     
  notebook Run: import mlflow                                                   
  mlflow.set_tracking_uri("http://mlflow.mlflow:80")                            
  mlflow.set_experiment("my-first-experiment") with                             
  mlflow.start_run(run_name="hello-mlflow"): mlflow.log_param("greeting",       
  "hello") mlflow.log_metric("score", 42.0) print("Done!") Minutes 9-12: See    
  it in MLflow Open https://mlflow.dhbm.kitt-inf.net Click on                   
  my-first-experiment See the run you just logged from JupyterHub Minutes       
  13-15: Submit a GPU workflow Go back to https://argo.dhbm.kitt-inf.net Click  
  Workflow Templates, select quantization-recipe-template Click Submit Watch    
  Karpenter provision a GPU node (2-5 min) and the quantization DAG execute     
  Feature Status Summary Feature Status Notes Argo Workflows — CPU DAGs         
  Validated Multiple successful runs with artifacts, parameters, and            
  conditional gates Argo Workflows — GPU DAGs Validated Full quantization       
