  audit; comprehensive error analysis-before-deploy; rejected-alternatives      
  documentation |\n  37 \n  38 ### 3.2 SOPs that imply automation (from §6)\n   
  39 \n  40 - SOP-1 Drift-Triggered Retraining → Drift detection + correlate +  
  reproduce + parallel-train + evaluate + register + propose-deploy + canary +  
  rollback + ORBIT append.\n  41 - SOP-2 New Experiment Cycle → Problem         
  framing + literature survey + pre-analysis + data audit + parallel design +   
  ablation + error analysis + recommend.\n  42 - SOP-3 Production Incident      
  Response → Circuit-breaker + ack-in-Slack + cross-correlate + status updates  
  + remediation + PIR.\n  43 \n  44 ## 4. Gap Analysis: Existing vs Needed\n    
  45 \n  46 Effectively all ML-stack capabilities are missing. Generic MCPs     
  (atlassian, bitbucket, slack, socrates, confluence) provide infrastructure    
  for communication, code, data lake, ticketing, docs — not the ML-specific     
  platform operations.\n  47 \n  48 ## 5. Grouping Strategy\n  49 \n  50 I      
  group new capabilities into platform-coherent buckets (sharing auth/API       
  client/SDK):\n  51 \n  52 1. Training & Experiment Tracking Cluster —         
  Databricks/ML Studio/ML Fabric + MLflow + HPO (shared Databricks/ML-Studio    
  auth & MLflow API). Includes skill encoding the SOP-2 experiment workflow +   
  AI-native standards (multi-seed, k-fold, ablations, mean±std, literature      
  synthesis).\n  53 2. Feature Store Cluster — Tecton/MLFS + Unity Catalog      
  (shared feature-store SDK + governance). Includes feature audit/coverage      
  skill.\n  54 3. Model Registry, Packaging, Serving & Deployment Cluster —     
  Tarot v3 + MSP SDK + TEAMServe + SageMaker + Triton + AI Gateway + Launchpad  
  + Spinnaker + Micros + feature flags (shared deploy/registry SDK surface).    
  Includes deployment-manifest skill + canary/rollback skill.\n  55 4.          
  Observability, SLO & Incident Response Cluster — ML Lens + SignalFx           
  (detectors-as-code) + circuit breakers + JSM Ops. Includes SOP-3              
  incident-response skill, SOP-1 drift-retraining skill, runbook execution      
  skill, PIR auto-drafting skill.\n  56 5. Evaluation Cluster — rovo-eval +     
  AIX-eval + ML Launch Review Checklist gate (shared eval framework surface).   
  Includes 6-stage evaluation skill + AI-native standards skill (exhaustive     
  failure analysis, calibration, slicing, fairness).\n  57 6. Cost / FinOps     
  Cluster — ML Artifact Cost Monitoring + FinOps queries + per-experiment       
  budget guardrails.\n  58 7. Symphony/ORBIT/Governance Cluster — ORBIT         
  context read/write + policy-pack enforcement + audit-log sink +               
  autonomy-tier gating + deployment-manifest schema. (Often platform-infra;     
  only the agent-side skill for using these is in scope.)\n  59 8.              
  Communication & Reporting Cluster — Weekly digests, model cards, experiment   
  reports, PIRs, standup digest, quarterly narrative — leverages existing       
  Slack/Confluence/Jira/Gmail MCPs but needs a skill encoding the templates     
  and cadence.\n  60 9. Association Subtask (mandatory) — Catalog existing      
  MCPs (atlassian, bitbucket, slack, socrates, confluence, google_drive,        
  gmail, etc.) and twg skill against role needs.\n  61 \n  62 ### 5.1           
  Consolidation to fit the "3 subtasks" guidance\n  63 \n  64 The               
  decomposition instructions state break into 3 focused subtasks. To respect    
  that while preserving the mandatory association subtask, I consolidate the    
  new-work into two large "creation" subtasks organized by responsibility       
  boundary:\n  65 \n  66 - Subtask 1 (CREATE — ML Lifecycle Core):              
  Training+Experimentation, Feature Store,                                      
  Registry+Packaging+Serving+Deployment. This bundles platforms that share the  
  "build-and-ship-a-model" boundary and reference common                        
  ML-Studio/Databricks/AWS auth. Includes the AI-native experiment &            
  deployment skills.\n  67 - Subtask 2 (CREATE — ML Ops, Reliability, Eval,     
  Cost & AI-Native Governance): Observability/SLO, Incident Response (SOP-3) &  
  drift-retraining (SOP-1), Evaluation Frameworks (rovo-eval/AIX-eval +         
  6-stage), Cost/FinOps, Symphony/ORBIT/Governance, Communication/Reporting.    
  This bundles the "run-and-prove-the-model" boundary, all sharing the          
  observability+governance audit surface.\n  68 - Subtask 3 (ASSOCIATE —        
  mandatory): Map every existing MCP toolset and skill to the role's            
  responsibilities; identify exactly what is and isn't covered.\n  69 \n  70    
  This grouping respects the platform-coherence rule (shared APIs/auth)         
  without merging unrelated capabilities: subtask 1 is "model build & ship",    
  subtask 2 is "model run, evaluate, govern & report". They have                
  non-overlapping scopes (Subtask 1 stops once the model is deployed; Subtask   
  2 starts at observe/eval/govern/communicate around live models).\n  71 \n     
  72 ## 6. Verification Against the Four Criteria\n  73 \n  74 1.               
  Comprehensive — All 14 capability clusters (A–N) and all 3 SOPs are covered:  
  clusters A/B/C/D in subtask 1; clusters E/F/G/H/I/J/M/N in subtask 2;         
  existing-tool mapping in subtask 3. Day-One Readiness (§11) items map into    
  subtasks 1 and 2.\n  75 2. Independent — Each subtask references a disjoint   
  set of platforms and deliverables. Subtask 1 ends at registry/deploy;         
  subtask 2 begins at observe/eval. Subtask 3 is read-only catalog.\n  76 3.    
  Non-conflicting — No shared files; deliverables target distinct skill/tool    
  directories. Subtask 3 (association) only reads; can run in parallel without  
  conflict.\n  77 4. Actionable — Each subtask has a clear scope statement, an  
  enumerated list of skills/tools to create (or catalog), and a research        
  requirement tied to specific role-doc sections.\n  78 \n  79 ## 7.            
  Aggregation Guidance\n  80 \n  81 The aggregator should:\n  82 1.             
  Consolidate the two creation subtask outputs into a single ordered roadmap    
  of skills/tools (CRITICAL first), de-duplicating any incidental overlap       
  (e.g., MLflow logging touched by both build and eval).\n  83 2. Cross-check   
  the creation subtasks against the association subtask's existing-coverage     
  map — promote any item the association subtask shows can be partially         
  satisfied by an existing MCP from "Create" to "Extend".\n  84 3. Produce a    
  final unified manifest: {skill_or_tool_name, type, platform, scope, status    
  (Create/Associate/Extend), priority, source role-doc reference}.\n  85 4.     
  Flag any infrastructure-level items (ORBIT, Symphony orchestrator engine,     
  audit-log sinks) into a gaps section since they are not tool/skill-level      
  concerns.\n  86 \n  87 ## 8. Known Gaps Outside Tool/Skill Scope\n  88 \n     
  89 These belong in gaps, not in subtasks:\n  90 - ORBIT context layer         
  storage / write API (platform infra).\n  91 - Symphony Orchestrator fleet     
  manager itself (platform infra).\n  92 - Policy pack version-control system   
  & approval workflow (platform infra).\n  93 - Audit log sink                  
  infrastructure.\n  94 - The "Policy Architect" and "Agent Reliability         
  Engineer" new-role companion agents (not tools for this role).\n  95          
