  ["artifacts/ml_studio_one_pager.txt:\n\n````text\n   0 :target-1: Mission     
  Maximize Atlassian teams’ productivity in developing and operating Machine    
  Learning models/pipelines and the value delivered to customers through        
  Machine Learning. 🔭 Vision In the future, Atlassian teams will achieve high  
  productivity in developing and operating Machine Learning models/pipelines.   
  The complexities related to ML data preparation and model training are        
  greatly simplified, all while ensuring strict adherence to data security and  
  privacy policies. Any Atlassian team that wants to create customized AI/ML    
  solutions can utilize an ML purpose-built development platform called ML      
  Studio . ML Studio enables teams to access and prepare high-quality ML        
  datasets, create end-to-end ML workflows, and efficiently conduct numerous    
  experiments. :chess: Strategy To achieve these goals, we aim to follow these  
  strategies: Streamline ML workflows by 1) promoting reusabilities of          
  privacy-compliant datasets, data processing, and training modules; 2) making  
  experimentation as lightweight and iterative as possible; and 3) Integrating  
  with a wide range of advanced AI infrastructures, framework, and open-source  
  models. Strong ML Engineer and platform partnership through cross-team        
  collaborations between Search (representing high-impact and ML-adept team)    
  and ML platform while designing and building ML Studio.                       
  image-20240315-105949.png Streamline ML Workflows Current complexities and    
  pain points of ML development in Atlassian arise from the lack of             
  high-quality ML datasets, privacy-compliant and reusable ML workflow          
  building blocks, built-in experimentation support, and powerful AI infra. ML  
  Studio aims to address these challenges by supporting: Reusable and           
  privacy-compliant data/module We aim to minimize the necessity for teams to   
  construct data pipelines from scratch by offering a range of raw,             
  intermediate, and final datasets generated through centralized pipelines.     
  Additionally, ML Studio will decrease the costs of pipeline composition and   
  mitigate privacy violation risks by providing a collection of data            
  processing and training modules that comply with privacy policies. Fast       
  experimentation In real-world ML development, model performance improvements  
  typically arise from rapid experimentation, feature engineering, and model    
  tuning rather than the application of fundamentally different algorithms.     
  Therefore, we’ll facilitate experimentation by automating experiment          
  creation and configuration/metrics tracking. We’ll also simplify the process  
  of discovering and replicating existing experiments to enable incremental     
  iterations. Integration with advanced AI infra, framework, and open-source    
  models The ML industry is actively developing more advanced AI                
  infrastructure that supports distributed and accelerated training of many     
  open-sourced large-scale DL models. ML Studio will be extensible in           
  integrating with various advanced AI infrastructures, AI frameworks,          
  optimization libraries, and open-source models available in the market. ML    
  Engineer + Platform Partnership Many big-tech companies learned the hard way  
  that ML engineers (MLE) and ML platform teams should collaborate early and    
  closely in defining and building ML development tools. In Atlassian, the      
  Search team is ML-adept and requires a productive ML development platform to  
  accelerate its ML progress. The ML platform team has the expertise and        
  motivation to build a suite of ML tools. By leveraging the power of these     
  two entities, the jointly constructed ML Studio will empower advanced ML use  
  cases and deliver continuous business impact. This success will also inspire  
  other teams interested in developing ML workflows to adopt the platform.      
  Search and MLP teams will collaborately design ML Studio’s user experience    
  and define the roadmap from the outset. Throughout the execution phase, the   
  Search team's primary focus will be on creating solutions for search use      
  cases, including reusable datasets and modules. Meanwhile, the ML platform    
  team will concentrate on addressing underlying challenges such as privacy     
  control, workflow orchestration, fast experimentation support, and AI infra   
  integration. 💡 Business Impact The business impacts of the ML Studio will    
  manifest through: Boosting MLE productivity ML Studio will directly           
  contribute to ML development productivity. We will measure the reduction in   
  developer turnaround time at each stage of ML development. Additionally, we   
  will track the number of ML models and experiments powered by ML Studio.      
  Improving AI quality with faster data and model iterations, we anticipate     
  continuous enhancements in AI feature quality powered by ML Studio. For       
  Search, we will measure the quantitative improvements in search relevance     
  driven by ML development iterations. Increasing AI MAU with the improved      
  quality of existing and the development of new AI features, we expect to see  
  a higher adoption rate among users for Atlassian AI features.                 
  image-20240315-105326.png :team: Current vs Ideal User Journey (You can       
  refer to this page for a more detailed breakdown and comparison of user       
  experiences) Data Exploration & Preparation Available Datasets Old            
  Experience While raw behavioral and UGC datasets are available in Socrates    
  and Plato, there are only limited ML datasets typically generated by ad-hoc   
  notebooks with no guarantee on data freshness or quality promises. New        
  Experience ML Studio will enable a collection of high-quality intermediate    
  and final ML datasets carrying features and labels generated by centralized   
  pipelines with built-in data quality monitoring. Data Exploration Old         
  Experience When MLEs need to analyze data, they’ll find that tables carrying  
  UGC data are entirely inaccessible from interactive notebooks. Instead, they  
  need to go through a rigorous process of privacy and code review to compose   
  jobs that can access UGC data. Additionally, they may find themselves         
  frequently switching between different Databrick workspaces due to varying    
  access control settings. New Experience Tables carrying UGC data will be      
  accessible from notebooks thanks to row-level and column-level ACL properly   
  configured for all ML datasets. This enables data exploration with minimal    
  or no privacy/code review costs, while also saving MLEs from switching        
  between Databrick workspaces. Data Preparation Old Experience When MLEs need  
  to prepare data for model training or evaluation, they’ll find there is lack  
  of reusable data preparation modules. Consequently, they have to start from   
  scratch most of the time which again include a privacy and code               
  review-intensive process to compose jobs that can access UGC data. Every      
  future data workflow change or new data workflow also needs MLP code review,  
  resulting significant overhead for both MLP and users. Furthermore, running   
  new or updated workflows requires Bitbucket branch deployment, which          
  unfortunately leads to frequent deployment conflicts that cause user          
  frustrations. New Experience MLEs can leverage reusable privacy-compliant     
  data processing modules (e.g., labeling) to compose data workflows.           
  Privacy/code review is typically needed for new data processing modules, but  
  is rarely needed for workflow composition/updates that use existing modules.  
  Running new/updated workflow can be submitted directly from CLI/GUI even      
  with locally-built modules. Once workflow is productionized, it will be       
  equipped with built-in data quality monitoring and alerting. Model Training   
  & Deployment Training/Evaluating Models Old Experience Very similar to that   
  of the data preparation experience and suffers the same painpoints.           
  Furthermore, the availability of GPU resources is unpredictable and there is  
  no native support for distributed training. New Experience MLEs can utilize   
  reusable privacy-compliant training modules to construct training workflows   
  and minimize the need for privacy/code review. The training process will be   
  supported by various advanced AI infrastructures from different providers     
  such as Databricks, MosaicML, and AWS Sagemaker . This approach increases     
  the likelihood of securing GPU resources and enables accelerated and          
  distributed training. Experimentation Old Experience MLEs are required to     
  write additional code to set up MLFlow experiment and tracking                
  configuration/metrics. The experiment runs cannot be reproduced from MLFlow,  
  unless it is powered by a notebook. New Experience An experiment is           
  automatically created or reused for each workflow run. The entire workflow    
  configuration and metrics are automatically tracked in each run. It is easy   
  to search and clone existing experiment runs, modify configurations, and      
  trigger new runs through CLI/GUI. This system also supports AutoML and        
  hyperparameter sweeping experiments to further increase MLE productivities.   
  Model deployment and continual training Old Experience MLEs need some         
  efforts to set up model deployment for each trained model. There is no        
  built-in support for continual training or deployment. New Experience Model   
  deployment is a reusable module to add to/remove from ML workflows and also   
  be directly triggered from experiment management CLI/GUI. Each ML workflow    
  includes built-in support for continual training and deployment, which can    
  be easily toggled on or off. :roadmap: Roadmap                                
  https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/3355085101 FY24 H2:         
  https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/3289787333 FY25 H1:         
  https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/3989449133 Closed Beta      
  launch:                                                                       
  https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/blog/2024/07/03/3915351305        
  :link: References ML development pain points:                                 
  https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202088d1945544364701b4921ae3c0748  
